title: Making the imported data available in Skg
pid: f7bbc08f-ed63-4b75-9be6-b22148a11892
body: |2-
  The command writes one 'ID.skg' file per resulting node. It does not edit
  configuration, start the server, or refresh a running server's graph and
  search index. 'REPO_NAME' labels the skgrepo during conversion; the permanent
  skgrepo association comes from the configured directory, not a skgrepo field
  inside each file.

  After reviewing the output, place it in the intended owned skgrepo directory
  and configure that skgrepo with the intended name and privacy order. See
  [[id:02708898-2d66-49cf-ba60-ecc1eb0bbe81][the configuration reference]]. Check for ID collisions with existing skgrepos
  before loading: the importer itself does not check them.

  For a first setup, configure the skgrepo before starting Skg. When replacing
  existing data, save and close the open views and stop the server before
  changing the files in its skgrepos, then restart it. If files in an already configured
  skgrepo change while the server runs, the server reloads them as they
  change, and rerenders the clean views that show them.
  'M-x skg-rebuild-session-lifetime-stores' reloads an edited config, a new
  skgrepo included.

  The Cargo command prints a reminder to commit the imported files so Git
  diff views have a baseline; it does not run Git. Reimporting replaces the
  destination and gives ID-less headlines new IDs, so it is not an incremental
  update workflow. Keep your originals and a backup of Skg edits you want to
  retain. [[id:b778e2a3-8aa2-43e7-a160-1bf648569b39][Exporting back to Org]] does not restore the original files or all graph
  semantics.
